# An alias moved from one known Structure to another before a path write: the write goes to the NEW target (the book,
# #frame-and-graph-assignment: a path write replaces the fragment of the field of the Structure the name denotes at that
# moment; the existing rows rebind the own place with `p := B`, and `@: payload leaf\data` binds an alias to a path).
# w is bound to shared\First, then `w := shared\Second`, then `w\x := 9`: Second's x is 9, First's x stays 1. First and
# Second differ in x, so a route that takes the target of the first initializer writes First and fails. The declaration
# of an alias does not prove its target after an assignment; a route that cannot prove it at translation may take the
# whole callable through the walker. Every route answers the same.
(
    First:
        int: x 1
    Second:
        int: x 2
)
sub: main ()
    @: shared node[0]
    @: w shared\First
    w := shared\Second
    w\x := 9
main()
